  "text": "As he was getting ready for the penalty shootout, he reached into his sock and pulled out a small piece of paper. That paper somehow contained exact directions of where each enemy player will shoot the ball. You see, as German goalkeeper Jens Lehmann was preparing for a tiebreaker of the biggest international game of his career yet, his manager slid him a cheat sheet with the names of seven Argentinian shooters and the directions in which they're most likely to shoot. Prior to this match, Germany's coaching staff spent countless hours analyzing their opponent's gameplay, and specifically, their penalty shooting tendencies. Julio Cruz stepped up first. Lehmann, yes, decided correctly, but was just shy of reaching the ball. Before the second shot, the keeper took a good look at his note, successfully predicted the direction, and caught the ball. Mind you, historically only about 20% of penalties are saved by the keeper. This is an incredible achievement of skill, luck, and in Germany's case, perhaps even analytical homework. The papers said that Rodriguez would shoot left. And what do you know? He does. But once again, Lehmann was just milliseconds behind. At this point, due to Germany's perfect shooting, one more save from Lehmann would send his team straight to the semifinals. He took a good look at the sheet, looked at Cambiasso, Dove left, and successfully saved it, instantly etching his name into the World Cup history. But that's not the complete story. See, the cheat sheet didn't have all the Argentinian players, in fact, the first and the last shooters were not on the list. He had no information about them, but still pretended to read the paper note as a mind game tactic, making the shooters paranoid like he knows exactly what they're about to do."
